\section{BA-Layer Modality Comparison}
\label{tab:ba_layer_comparison}

To address the concern that threshold-dependent layer selection may circularly favor HS probes, we repeat the modality comparison at layers selected by pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric (\cref{tab:ba_layer_results}). BA-selected layers fall at middle depth (L9--L47), distinct from the deep layers (L60--L63) chosen by threshold-dependent metrics.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{HS vs.\ text at BA-selected layers. All 16 model-metric comparisons pass Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$), refuting the circularity concern. BA-L: layer selected by pure balanced accuracy.}
\label{tab:ba_layer_results}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{BA-L} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & $\Delta$\textbf{AUROC} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L12 & +17.8 (.004) & +7.8 (.004) & $-$12.9 (.004) & +9.3 (.004) \\
OT-7B & L9 & +25.4 (.004) & +11.7 (.004) & $-$19.3 (.004) & +14.8 (.004) \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & L30 & +18.0 (.006) & +14.8 (.004) & $-$8.0 (.041) & +17.3 (.004) \\
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & L47 & +13.7 (.004) & +14.9 (.004) & $-$23.1 (.004) & +21.1 (.004) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\begin{flushleft}
\scriptsize Values: difference in pp ($p$-value, bootstrap 10{,}000 resamples). $\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$).
\end{flushleft}
\end{table}

All 16 tests (4 models $\times$ 4 metrics) pass Holm-Bonferroni correction. The precision gains (+13.7 to +25.4pp) are comparable to those at threshold-dependent layers (+15.4 to +32.5pp), confirming that the HS advantage is robust to the choice of layer selection criterion. QwQ-32B FPR ($p{=}0.041$) is the weakest individual test but still passes its step-down threshold.


\section{Post-Hoc Power Analysis}
\label{app:power_analysis}

We compute post-hoc minimum detectable effects (MDEs) at 80\% power for each model's test set to contextualize non-significant results, particularly at 32B scale where sample sizes are small (\cref{tab:power_analysis}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Post-hoc power analysis. MDE: minimum detectable effect (balanced accuracy, pp) at 80\% power given each model's test-set size and observed variance. Effects smaller than the MDE cannot be reliably detected.}
\label{tab:power_analysis}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & $n_{\text{test}}$ & \textbf{Obs.\ std (BA)} & \textbf{MDE (80\%)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & 43 & 0.028 & 4.2pp \\
OT-7B & 47 & 0.023 & 3.3pp \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & 19 & 0.028 & 8.6pp \\
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & 20 & 0.056 & 9.5pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\begin{flushleft}
\scriptsize $\dagger$\,Sample-limited. MDE computed from 5-seed observed standard deviation via two-sided paired $t$-test formula.
\end{flushleft}
\end{table}

At 32B scale, the MDE is 8.6--9.5pp, meaning effects below this magnitude cannot be reliably distinguished from noise. R1-32B's residualized HS-text gap (+1.9pp) falls well below the MDE (9.5pp), so the non-significant result (\cref{app:position_residual}) is consistent with either a true null or an underpowered test. The 7--8B MDEs (3.3--4.2pp) are substantially lower, explaining why smaller effects reach significance at these scales.

\paragraph{High false positive rate and oracle gap.}
Safe-trace FPR is 39--52\% at standard thresholds, so current probes characterize the phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors. The 85.5\% oracle gap (computed on R1-8B only; \cref{sec:oracle}) confirms that passive probing captures only a fraction of available signal.

\paragraph{Post-hoc power at 32B.}
At $n{=}19$--$20$, the minimum detectable effect at 80\% power is 8.6--9.5pp (\cref{app:power_analysis}). R1-32B's residualized HS-text gap (+1.9pp) falls well below this threshold, so non-significance is consistent with either a true null or insufficient power. All 32B conclusions marked $\dagger$ should be interpreted with this constraint.

